WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.600 --> 00:00:02.602
-Our first guest tonight
is an Emmy Award-winning

00:00:02.602 --> 00:00:03.936
comedian and actor

00:00:03.936 --> 00:00:06.806
who is in his record-breaking
21st season on "SNL."

00:00:06.806 --> 00:00:09.509
His memoir,
"When I Was Your Age:

00:00:09.509 --> 00:00:10.709
Life Lessons, Funny Stories,

00:00:10.709 --> 00:00:11.878
and Questionable
Parenting Advice

00:00:11.878 --> 00:00:13.846
from a Professional Clown,"
is available now.

00:00:13.846 --> 00:00:16.916
And "SNL" returns live on NBC
and Peacock this Saturday

00:00:16.916 --> 00:00:18.885
with host Dakota Johnson
and musical guest

00:00:18.885 --> 00:00:19.953
Justin Timberlake.

00:00:19.953 --> 00:00:21.554
Please welcome back to the show,

00:00:21.554 --> 00:00:24.457
one of the all-time greats,
Kenan Thompson!

00:00:24.457 --> 00:00:27.393
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:27.393 --> 00:00:43.242 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:43.242 --> 00:00:45.878
-My friend! My old friend.

00:00:45.878 --> 00:00:47.446
-'Sup, everybody.

00:00:47.446 --> 00:00:48.848
[ Applause ]
Hello, everyone.

00:00:48.848 --> 00:00:53.719
Well, well, well.

00:00:53.719 --> 00:00:55.221
Thank you.

00:00:55.221 --> 00:00:56.989
-This is so exciting,
to see you here.

00:00:56.989 --> 00:00:58.258
-What a nice reception.

00:00:58.258 --> 00:00:59.426
-We've seen you here
for over two decades.

00:00:59.426 --> 00:01:01.227
It is still exciting,
though, to...

00:01:01.227 --> 00:01:03.496
-You're also right there.
-I know. I'm right here.

00:01:03.496 --> 00:01:04.563
-"SNL" is, like, right there.

00:01:04.563 --> 00:01:05.732
-We don't see
each other, though.

00:01:05.732 --> 00:01:06.766
I mean, because again,
when you're right there,

00:01:06.766 --> 00:01:07.833
you're doing your thing

00:01:07.833 --> 00:01:08.834
When I'm here,
I'm doing my thing.

00:01:08.834 --> 00:01:10.003
-Very true, very true.

00:01:10.003 --> 00:01:11.538
But I also, that room
right down the hall,

00:01:11.538 --> 00:01:14.006
where we both
auditioned for "SNL."

00:01:14.006 --> 00:01:17.143
You talk about your audition
in this wonderful book.

00:01:17.143 --> 00:01:19.545
It seems crazy
to even look back and think

00:01:19.545 --> 00:01:21.581
there was a time
where "SNL" was weighing,

00:01:21.581 --> 00:01:24.283
"Do we want Kenan Thompson?
Do we not want Kenan Thompson?"

00:01:24.283 --> 00:01:26.719
But you said said,
one of the nice things is,

00:01:26.719 --> 00:01:28.421
you could hear
certain laughs in your audition.

00:01:28.421 --> 00:01:29.588
-Yeah.

00:01:29.588 --> 00:01:31.824
-That was actually helpful
to be able to,

00:01:31.824 --> 00:01:34.860
like, know ahead of time,
being a fan of the show,

00:01:34.860 --> 00:01:36.795
like, how people laugh

00:01:36.795 --> 00:01:39.298
so I could tell
who was laughing.

00:01:39.298 --> 00:01:42.301
Lorne has a very kind of like,
master of the universe laugh.

00:01:42.301 --> 00:01:45.071
-Yeah.
-Very low and guttural

00:01:45.071 --> 00:01:46.472
when he really finds
something funny.

00:01:46.472 --> 00:01:47.740
[ Imitates laugh ]

00:01:47.740 --> 00:01:49.842
Alright. That's pretty good.

00:01:49.842 --> 00:01:53.579
Tina, Tina Fey is, like,
a little more of a cackle.

00:01:53.579 --> 00:01:55.548
It kind of explodes out of her.

00:01:55.548 --> 00:01:56.983
-That's a very good way of
putting it.

00:01:56.983 --> 00:01:58.050
-Because I guess she listens

00:01:58.050 --> 00:01:59.319
to a lot of jokes
and stuff like that.

00:01:59.319 --> 00:02:00.586
So when she finds
something actually funny --

00:02:00.586 --> 00:02:01.721
[ Imitates laugh ]

00:02:01.721 --> 00:02:03.756
All right, that's good.

00:02:03.756 --> 00:02:05.224
Poehler is a good laugher.

00:02:05.224 --> 00:02:07.460
She really --
She's like a kid laughing.

00:02:07.460 --> 00:02:09.061
[ Imitates laugh ]

00:02:09.061 --> 00:02:10.530
You know, that kind of thing.

00:02:10.530 --> 00:02:12.398
-She's one of the best, one of
the most supportive laughers.

00:02:12.398 --> 00:02:15.735
-Big time, loves the fact that
that was just funny, basically.

00:02:15.735 --> 00:02:16.802
That's her thing.

00:02:16.802 --> 00:02:19.472
Maya is like
a proud mom, you know?

00:02:19.472 --> 00:02:21.841
[ Imitates laugh ]

00:02:21.841 --> 00:02:23.709
It's like,
"Oh, Maya. That's nice.

00:02:23.709 --> 00:02:25.044
She's a goddess."

00:02:25.044 --> 00:02:27.346
And you have
a very funny laugh.

00:02:27.346 --> 00:02:29.682
-You know, it's interesting.
I noticed in the book,

00:02:29.682 --> 00:02:32.885
you said, "Google 'Muttley.'"
-Yeah.

00:02:32.885 --> 00:02:34.687
-Which is a Hanna-Barbera.
I'm guessing?

00:02:34.687 --> 00:02:35.921
-It is.

00:02:35.921 --> 00:02:38.090
It's a reference
to the dog in Hanna-Barbera

00:02:38.090 --> 00:02:39.992
that was very sneaky.

00:02:39.992 --> 00:02:43.362
-And I will say,
I took your advice

00:02:43.362 --> 00:02:44.596
and I said I was
going to Google it.

00:02:44.596 --> 00:02:46.032
When I watched, I was like,
"Oh, son of a gun.

00:02:46.032 --> 00:02:48.501
I do laugh like that."
-Yes, you do.

00:02:48.501 --> 00:02:49.936
-Show the audience.
-Do we have it?

00:02:49.936 --> 00:02:53.373
-This is how I laughed in our
years together at the table.

00:02:53.373 --> 00:02:54.740
-It's only because
you're a little rascal.

00:02:54.740 --> 00:02:56.208
Let's show it.

00:02:56.208 --> 00:02:58.278
-[ Cackling ]

00:03:01.280 --> 00:03:03.949
-That is.
That is kind of my laugh.

00:03:03.949 --> 00:03:07.754
[ Applause ]
-Yeah.

00:03:07.754 --> 00:03:09.655
-I did. It was so funny.
I was a little rascal.

00:03:09.655 --> 00:03:12.191
-Yeah. It's like a little rascal
just found something funny.

00:03:12.191 --> 00:03:13.826
[ Imitates laugh ]

00:03:13.826 --> 00:03:15.128
-Yeah, I love it.

00:03:15.128 --> 00:03:17.629
-Some of my favorite
things about this book

00:03:17.629 --> 00:03:19.065
is not just your history
in comedy,

00:03:19.065 --> 00:03:20.866
but also you talk about
your two wonderful daughters.

00:03:20.866 --> 00:03:24.603
Very funny story about
going to your daughter Georgia

00:03:24.603 --> 00:03:26.538
with help for a password.

00:03:26.538 --> 00:03:28.307
-Yeah, I don't know
if you know this or not,

00:03:28.307 --> 00:03:31.644
but children and technology
are really like this these days.

00:03:31.644 --> 00:03:36.048
She is prolific and I am not.

00:03:36.048 --> 00:03:38.116
And I went to her, asking her
for help for, like,

00:03:38.116 --> 00:03:39.218
a log-in or something.

00:03:39.218 --> 00:03:41.053
I was like, "Do I need to, like,

00:03:41.053 --> 00:03:42.588
forget my password and get
an email and do all that?"

00:03:42.588 --> 00:03:43.956
She was like, "Bruh,
let me just show you."

00:03:43.956 --> 00:03:47.526
I was like,
"Did you just call me bruh?"

00:03:47.526 --> 00:03:48.994
[ Laughs ]

00:03:48.994 --> 00:03:52.031
Yeah, but that's what
happens with a 9-year-old

00:03:52.031 --> 00:03:54.066
and you're getting on
their nerves.

00:03:54.066 --> 00:03:56.302
-You did say there is
one thing that would be worse

00:03:56.302 --> 00:03:58.437
than if your daughter
called you "bruh."

00:03:58.437 --> 00:03:59.571
-Which is...
-"Kenan."

00:03:59.571 --> 00:04:00.873
-Oh, yeah.

00:04:00.873 --> 00:04:03.508
No, I can't do the first name
thing for children.

00:04:03.508 --> 00:04:05.711
No. Show some respect.

00:04:05.711 --> 00:04:07.079
-Yeah.
-It's "Dad."

00:04:07.079 --> 00:04:09.983
-Can you imagine as a kid
calling -- even other parents,

00:04:09.983 --> 00:04:11.350
who said, "Call me
by my first name."

00:04:11.350 --> 00:04:13.886
-If I called my dad "Fletcher"?

00:04:13.886 --> 00:04:17.790
Bro, I'd be across the room
in two seconds

00:04:17.790 --> 00:04:19.391
with a foot to the face.

00:04:19.391 --> 00:04:21.127
-You would be like,
"I had no idea

00:04:21.127 --> 00:04:22.328
how fast you move, Fletcher."

00:04:22.328 --> 00:04:27.099
-Yeah. Word.
"Good on you, Fletch."

00:04:27.099 --> 00:04:28.534
Yeah, I love them.
They were just here.

00:04:28.534 --> 00:04:30.736
We saw Justin Timberlake
do his music rehearsal just now.

00:04:30.736 --> 00:04:32.772
-With your daughters?
Yeah, they came to check it out.

00:04:32.772 --> 00:04:34.273
-And he took a picture.
He's the nicest.

00:04:34.273 --> 00:04:36.208
-That's really exciting.
-It's really great.

00:04:36.208 --> 00:04:40.212
-I mean, you now, finally,
your daughters are like,

00:04:40.212 --> 00:04:41.614
kids get old enough
where you can actually now

00:04:41.614 --> 00:04:43.582
be a gatekeeper
to things they find cool.

00:04:43.582 --> 00:04:45.484
-Absolutely,
and it makes me cool.

00:04:45.484 --> 00:04:46.986
-Yeah.
-It makes me feel good.

00:04:46.986 --> 00:04:49.689
It makes me feel good
to be cool in my kids' eyes.

00:04:49.689 --> 00:04:52.725
-Yeah.
My kids met Forte this summer.

00:04:52.725 --> 00:04:53.826
You're kidding. How was that?

00:04:53.826 --> 00:04:55.127
Of course,
kids love Will Forte, right?

00:04:55.127 --> 00:04:56.462
-Come on. Who doesn't?

00:04:56.462 --> 00:04:59.131
-And then they were on Hulu
messing around,

00:04:59.131 --> 00:05:01.367
and they stumbled on
"Last Man on Earth,"

00:05:01.367 --> 00:05:02.601
and they said, "What is this?"

00:05:02.601 --> 00:05:06.005
And I said, "This is
a documentary about Will."

00:05:06.005 --> 00:05:08.407
-Also at the end of the world.
It is a documentary.

00:05:08.407 --> 00:05:10.877
-Will went through a bad time
before he met you.

00:05:10.877 --> 00:05:12.444
-It was still one
of the greatest shows.

00:05:12.444 --> 00:05:13.712
It's the greatest.
-Incredible.

00:05:13.712 --> 00:05:15.081
-Shout out to Will Forte.

00:05:15.081 --> 00:05:18.217
-Shout out to Will Forte,
making multi-generations laugh.

00:05:18.217 --> 00:05:21.020
-Absolutely.
-21 years.

00:05:21.020 --> 00:05:22.421
Hard to wrap your head around.

00:05:22.421 --> 00:05:24.289
One of, of course, the most
iconic things on the show,

00:05:24.289 --> 00:05:26.158
which you've been lucky enough
to do a lot of times,

00:05:26.158 --> 00:05:27.927
is scream "Live from New York."

00:05:27.927 --> 00:05:29.695
I would imagine
that never gets old.

00:05:29.695 --> 00:05:33.032
I did it a handful of times
and it was always exciting.

00:05:33.032 --> 00:05:34.600
-It doesn't get old,

00:05:34.600 --> 00:05:38.771
and it also doesn't have a fame
level to where it's not cool.

00:05:38.771 --> 00:05:41.074
I've seen like, Obama do it

00:05:41.074 --> 00:05:43.442
and just really be tickled
by the fact that he's doing it.

00:05:43.442 --> 00:05:44.510
You know what I mean?

00:05:44.510 --> 00:05:45.911
And anybody else that has,

00:05:45.911 --> 00:05:48.014
you know, uber-uber-fame,
you know,

00:05:48.014 --> 00:05:49.515
McCartney's done it,
Mick Jagger's hosted.

00:05:49.515 --> 00:05:51.183
All these people

00:05:51.183 --> 00:05:53.986
that have had the opportunity
to say that line.

00:05:53.986 --> 00:05:55.554
There's just something
about it, it's so historic.

00:05:55.554 --> 00:05:57.956
It's so beloved
and you know that.

00:05:57.956 --> 00:05:59.859
And you're also presenting,
you know,

00:05:59.859 --> 00:06:01.461
a good time to the country.

00:06:01.461 --> 00:06:04.730
-And everybody's cheering
after you say it.

00:06:04.730 --> 00:06:06.532
-Yeah, yeah.
It's never like, you drop it.

00:06:06.532 --> 00:06:08.200
It doesn't hit
the ground at all.

00:06:08.200 --> 00:06:11.370
No, "Live from New York,"
it's like, "Yeah!"

00:06:11.370 --> 00:06:12.705
It's never like,
"Live from New York."

00:06:12.705 --> 00:06:15.074
-"This is Meet the Press!"
Nobody would yell.

00:06:15.074 --> 00:06:16.509
-No, just....

00:06:16.509 --> 00:06:17.576
-And that's been on
for a long time, too,

00:06:17.576 --> 00:06:19.445
but nobody gives a [bleep].
-No.

00:06:19.445 --> 00:06:23.015
Nobody gives a --

00:06:23.015 --> 00:06:25.184
-Don't tell Fletcher I cursed.
-I won't.

00:06:25.184 --> 00:06:26.686
-I have a lot more
questions for you.

00:06:26.686 --> 00:06:28.721
We'll be right back
with more Kenan after this.

